==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 07 NOTES — nmap Part 4
Date: 05-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- NSE (Nmap Scripting Engine) runs extra scripts against open ports for deeper info
- -sC runs the DEFAULT set of safe scripts; --script=<name> runs one specific script
- Scripts live in /usr/share/nmap/scripts/ - hundreds are installed by default
- -oN saves normal (readable) output, -oX saves XML (for other tools), -oA saves all formats at once
- XML output isn't meant for humans to read directly - it's structured for other programs to parse
- A combined -sV -sC -F scan, saved with -oN, is a realistic "first pass" recon command

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] nmap -sV -sC -p <ports> <target>
[ ] nmap --script=banner -p 22 <target>
[ ] nmap --script=http-title -p 80 <target>
[ ] nmap <target> -oN / -oX / -oA
[ ] a combined recon-style scan

paste your real output here :
$ nmap -sV -sC -p 22,80 scanme.nmap.org
> 

$ nmap --script=http-title -p 80 scanme.nmap.org
> 

$ nmap -sV -sC -F scanme.nmap.org -oN final_recon.txt
> 


